lement with id notice"'::jsonb, '"That paragraph because it has class notice"'::jsonb, '"All paragraphs only if they lack classes"'::jsonb, '"CSS"'::jsonb, '"TCP"'::jsonb, '"FTP only"'::jsonb, '"UDP"'::jsonb, '"Radio buttons in one named group"'::jsonb, '"A submit button only"'::jsonb, '"Checkboxes"'::jsonb, '"A single text heading"'::jsonb, '"Increase animation speed for everyone"'::jsonb, '"Replace animation with repeated blinking text"'::jsonb, '"Ignore the user preference"'::jsonb, '"Use a reduced-motion media query to reduce/disable nonessential animation"'::jsonb, '"UDP"'::jsonb, '"HTML"'::jsonb, '"HTTPS"'::jsonb, '"TCP"'::jsonb, '"The CSS box model stops working"'::jsonb, '"HTML elements stop being parsed"'::jsonb, '"DNS converts the password into a domain"'::jsonb, '"Credentials may be exposed in transit"'::jsonb, '"TLS protection for data exchanged between client and server"'::jsonb, '"Automatic correction of invalid HTML"'::jsonb, '"A mandatory CSS grid layout"'::jsonb, '"Guaranteed correctness of every page statement"'::jsonb, '"The table tfoot element"'::jsonb, '"@keyframes with animation properties"'::jsonb, '"HTML alt text"'::jsonb, '"The FTP protocol"'::jsonb, '"Margin is the text colour; padding is the font"'::jsonb, '"Padding is outside the element; margin is inside content"'::jsonb, '"Margin is outside the border; padding is between content and border"'::jsonb, '"Both are always drawn as visible borders"'::jsonb, '"teal because a class selector is more specific than a type selector"'::jsonb, '"Neither rule can match an h1"'::jsonb, '"black because type selectors always win"'::jsonb, '"Both colours are blended"'::jsonb, '"CSS"'::jsonb, '"DNS"'::jsonb, '"HTML"'::jsonb, '"FTP"'::jsonb, '"https://example.com/"'::jsonb, '"submit"'::jsonb, '"#heading"'::jsonb, '"style.css"'::jsonb, '"relative"'::jsonb, '"absolute with no positioned ancestor always"'::jsonb, '"fixed"'::jsonb, '"static only"'::jsonb, '"IPv6 is a CSS stylesheet type"'::jsonb, '"IPv4 uses 128 bits and IPv6 uses 32"'::jsonb, '"IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses; IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses"'::jsonb, '"Both versions are HTML tags"'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY['1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '3'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '1'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '0'::jsonb, '2'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Basics of Web Designing | ESE | medium | TestId 4400093531020129 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (4400093531020129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply hyperlinks and the anchor element in a practical situation such as linking a navigation menu to another page or a section on the same page. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply html tables, headings and cell spanning in a practical situation such as displaying a timetable with a heading spanning two columns. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply html forms and controls in a practical situation such as collecting a student''s name, choice and confirmation. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply css rules, selectors and property declarations in a practical situation such as applying one style to every paragraph and a separate style to one class. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nThe anchor element (`a`) creates a hyperlink and its `href` attribute supplies the destination. An absolute URL identifies a full address; a relative URL is resolved in relation to the current document or site structure. Fragment identifiers can link to an element with a matching `id`. Link text should describe the destination, and links should be checked for broken paths. Use links for navigation rather than styling ordinary text to look clickable, and distinguish navigation from buttons that perform an action.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn HTML table uses `table` as its container, `tr` for rows, and `th` or `td` for header and data cells. `thead`, `tbody` and `tfoot` group relevant parts of the table. `colspan` makes a cell span multiple columns; `rowspan` makes it span multiple rows. These attributes change the table grid, so the number and arrangement of cells must remain consistent. Tables should represent tabular data rather than general page layout, and header cells should clearly identify the meaning of each column or row.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA `form` groups controls and defines how submitted data is sent. `input` supports different controls such as text, radio, checkbox, hidden and submit/reset inputs; `select` provides a drop-down list. Each relevant control needs a meaningful name, and labels should identify controls clearly. Radio buttons are suited to one choice from a group; checkboxes allow independent selections. Client-side validation improves usability but should not replace server-side validation. Sensitive information should be transmitted over HTTPS and processed securely.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA CSS rule has a selector and a declaration block. Each declaration contains a property and value, for example `color: navy;`. Type selectors target elements, class selectors begin with a dot, and ID selectors begin with a hash. Selectors determine which elements match; the cascade, specificity and source order help decide which declaration applies when rules conflict. Keep declarations syntactically valid, use reusable classes for repeated styling, and avoid relying on unnecessarily specific selectors that make future changes difficult.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- Bharatiya Knowledge System | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 440099110101015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (440099110101015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A student must apply meaning, scope and classification of bharatiya knowledge systems in a practical situation such as organizing a course presentation across language, philosophy, science and technology. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply caturdasa-vidyasthana and the traditional knowledge disciplines in a practical situation such as classifying a Sanskrit grammar lesson within the traditional framework. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply the four vedas and their broad roles in a practical situation such as distinguishing a hymn collection from a collection associated with melodies. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A student must apply vedangas and their role in studying vedic texts in a practical situation such as choosing the relevant discipline for pronunciation, metre or grammar. Explain the method or reasoning, set out the main steps, and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nBharatiya Knowledge Systems (BKS) refers to knowledge traditions developed and transmitted in the Indian subcontinent across intellectual, practical, cultural and philosophical fields. Depending on the syllabus framework, areas include Vedic and philosophical literature, language sciences, mathematics, astronomy, medicine, arts, engineering, architecture and environmental practices. Classification helps organize sources and disciplines without treating every tradition as identical. A sound explanation names the field, identifies its historical or textual context, and distinguishes documented practices from later interpretation. Claims should be supported by the relevant text, artifact or historical evidence rather than generalized to all people or periods.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nCaturdasa-Vidyasthana is a traditional classification of fourteen knowledge disciplines. A common presentation counts the four Vedas, six Vedangas and four additional knowledge traditions such as Mimamsa, Nyaya, Purana and Dharmashastra, though descriptions can vary by source. The framework shows how textual study, interpretation, language, ritual, reasoning and normative traditions were organized. When explaining it, identify the components, show how the categories relate, and note that the scheme is a traditional taxonomy rather than a modern university department list.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe four Vedas are Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda and Atharvaveda. The Rigveda is known for hymns; the Samaveda is closely associated with chants and melodies; the Yajurveda contains prose formulas and material related to ritual performance; and the Atharvaveda includes hymns and material concerning everyday life and ritual. Their contents overlap and their textual traditions are complex, so descriptions should be broad rather than absolute. Explain each name and its principal association, and avoid attributing every later scientific or social idea directly to all four Vedas.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nThe six Vedangas are Shiksha (phonetics/pronunciation), Chandas (metre), Vyakarana (grammar), Nirukta (etymology or explanation of words), Kalpa (ritual procedure) and Jyotisha (astronomical/calendrical timing). They support accurate recitation, interpretation and performance connected with Vedic literature. They are complementary: phonetics helps pronunciation, grammar analyzes forms, metre studies poetic structure, and Jyotisha relates to timekeeping. A complete answer names the six fields and explains at least one practical role of each instead of confusing them with modern engineering subjects.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should connect the concept to the stated example, explain the steps in order and justify the outcome."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

